hting is good to get a general  sense of the topics that you don't know  enough about yet then the second thing I  would do is after I've read this paper  and I've looked at all these topics I  would look up all these topics or on the  Internet or I would look in detail at  the explanation in the textbook then I  would write down what these topics mean  in my own words such that I could  explain it to myself in the future then  the next step is to close your eyes and  to kind of talk to yourself as if you're  explaining to yourself the entire paper  and this may sound a little bit insane  you can also do it Out Loud by the way  but if you're in a public environment  this is maybe not the most handy but I  would kind of just close my eyes and try  to explain to myself the concept and  something that I've noticed with  technical subjects is that a lot of  times you think you understand all the  details but if you actually try to  explain it to yourself step by step in  your own words you often will stumble  and you will often find that there are  little details that you are not  comfortable with and so then I would  review again and the details that I'm  not comfortable with and I would go  again to re-explaining it to myself then  the next step what I would do is or I  would find a friend that is lying around  or I would just imagine a friend and I  would actually teach it to someone else  so I would actually be speaking out loud  and I usually did this in my room  because of course you cannot just speak  loud in a library and I would actually  try to teach all the concepts that I've  just learned about to someone else  speaking out loud and I think this is  actually another barrier to learning  that by trying to reproduce what we've  learned in our words and speaking them  we will actually find that a lot of  concepts are still a little bit fuzzy  and something that I would also do with  technical subjects is that if you for  example have a figure in the paper that  is quite important I would try to redraw  this figure from memory and explain  every little detail in this figure and  by doing this this has a few benefits  you and become really good at explaining  actually and teaching things to others  but also something that I've noticed is  that you get a really deep understanding  of the material and of course I would  only do this for subjects that are  really important and that I want to get  a deep understanding of because this  process takes quite long but I've  noticed with difficult technical  subjects doing it step by step like this  really works and it also especially  allows you to pinpoint where in the  process of explaining the technical  subject you have some missing  information because a lot of times when  we feel we do not understand as  technical subjects it's not that we  don't understand the entire message but  it's usually a certain word or a certain  concept that we haven't clarified to  ourselves yet and then the last thing I  would do and this is more for the memory  part I would say the key formulas and  the key figures and I would put them in  one page for example in notion or one  page in a Word document and that would  kind of be my summary and something that  I've noticed is that actually just look  looking at that page with those figures  and those formulas I could explain the  entire paper or the chapter by heart and  not because I memorized it but because I  know the concepts in the paper I have I  was able to explain it to other people  and and this actually really allows you  to get a deep understanding of the  technical subjects as well as remember  them when you need to and actually  something that I've noticed is that the  way I learn technical subjects is  actually quite similar to the way I've  learned languages so with languages I  actually do the same so I make a list of  the words for example and then from day  one I try to speak with someone or try  to reproduce What I've learned and 