 or a textbook and I've read the entire  concept I would look up immediately if  there is accompanying code for the  textbook or a company in code for the  paper so a lot of times you can actually  find code accompanying a paper on GitHub  so also with this paper that I showed  you before  um the code is online and it's called  gradient analysis and when I was  learning a little bit about this paper  and about the concepts they use I also  decided to run the code on my own  computer to see if it worked the way I  wanted to and if I could reproduce some  of their results and I think this is  something that usually really separates  technical subjects from other subjects  is that you also need to apply it and  the only way you can really get better  at technical subjects and applying  technical subjects is just by doing it  every day so something I did when I was  learning mathematics for my Physics  degree was that every morning I would  just set aside two hours to only do math  derivations and that's because I found  math derivations needed a certain part  of focus in a certain part of your brain  so just doing this every day really will  allow you to increase your abilities in  these technical subjects and then the  last part of my three-step process is  sharing the knowledge and personally  this is something I actually learned  mostly during my PhD is that not only  having the knowledge and being able to  apply the knowledge but also sharing  this knowledge is actually a key  component of of the learning process so  something that Austin Cleon said is that  make stuff you love and talk about the  stuff you love and you'll attract the  people who love that kind of stuff it's  that simple and I really love this kind  of concept I think if you share the  knowledge that you accumulate you will  actually gather around you a community  of people that also like to have the  same knowledge as you and that would  also like to accumulate the same  knowledge as you so I think as soon as  you start on the quest to learning a  certain technical subject for example  machine learning or computational  Neuroscience it's actually really nice  to share the knowledge that you've  learned and this can be done in really  small little steps so for example you  could share a tweet every day of a  certain Paper you read or you can write  a small article on medium or write a  part of your code Solution on stack  Overflow and I think by sharing your  knowledge you will actually see that a  lot of people will come back to you with  more knowledge and at a certain point  you actually almost don't have to do  anything anymore to accumulate more  knowledge because people just kind of  give it to you which is really nice and  I think this is really the benefit of  building a community around certain  topics that you love so these were the  tips I have for studying technical  subjects if you have some amazing  techniques as well as to how you learned  technical subjects are also languages I  would be really interested in it so  please put it down in the comments below  and otherwise see you next week bye

